From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:07:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: m4: Cache a static frame while nothing is flipping

With a static image on the J713 panel, the display keeps scanning the
frame out of memory 60 times a second. The DCP can instead cache it
(IdleCachingMethod 2), but only after the host registers a cache surface:
a swap with intent 8 whose sole surface is a separate surface object.
Setting the method alone does nothing.

Once no flip has followed for idle_cache_ms (default 1000, 0 disables),
set the method and submit that swap with a dedicated, retained surface
of the panel geometry, which the host never writes. The DCP keeps
presenting the frame on screen, steps through its idle states (D117) and
asks for a fence (D578) on entry and on each later refresh of the cache,
granted for the surface this host registered; the next present,
brightness change or blank leaves the cache (D579, then state 0). The
surface stays registered across exits, so later entries resubmit it.
After a rollback without a flip, entry is retried after ten quiet
periods. The surface is allocated at startup; without it, or if the DCP
refuses the method, the cache stays off. debugfs reports the idle state,
open fences and the number of entries.

On the J713, idle on battery with the backlight off, this saves 335 mW
(3.27 -> 2.93 W) within one run. Brightness changes, DPMS off/on and
suspend-to-idle leave and re-enter the cache.

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
(cherry picked from commit a150a02ca3534fb4debac9aa850179ae9c280240)
[dylan: the tools/testing/selftests/drivers/gpu/apple-dcp/m4.py hunk is
